    "summary": "Soaring Dark Star, epic Mountains of the Moon, fiery Alligator jam",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a soaring Dark Star and an epic Mountains Of The Moon that flow into a powerful second‑set climax. Highlights include The Other One, Dupree’s Diamond Blues, The Eleven and an intense Alligator > Drums > Alligator > Caution jam. Recording quality is excellent on the Goodbear soundboard.",
    "review": "Philadelphia’s Electric Factory on February 15, 1969 captured the Dead at a creative peak. From the opening rag the band built momentum into a soaring Dark Star that many reviewers likened to Mahavishnu Orchestra intensity, while the Mountains Of The Moon segued seamlessly into it with studio‑like precision. The second set turned up the heat: The Other One stretched out over cosmic terrain, Dupree's Diamond Blues provided a mellow prelude to an explosive Alligator > Drums > Alligator > Caution sequence drenched in feedback and snarl, and The Eleven showcased the group's rhythmic daring. Despite a few tape cuts (notably Lovelight), the Goodbear soundboard capture is crisp and clear, making this one of the finest documented performances from that era.\n\nJerry’s tone shines throughout, especially on Dark Star and the intro to Death Don't Have No Mercy, while Phil’s bass locks in with him during the extended jams. Mickey’s drumming adds a snarl and rhythmic interplay that drives the intensity, complemented by Bill’s steady pulse. The organ work underpins the improvisations, adding texture without overpowering the guitars. Overall, this concert stands out as a benchmark of 1969‑era Dead magic.",
